# Chapter 2: A Wish

> Symphony No. 5 in C minor (First Movement) - Ludwig van Beethoven

    Rain came down heavy on him.  Water climbed up to his boots, but the blue glow continued to beckon.  As he grew closer, the glow also moved closer to him.  The intense wind drowned out his calls, "What are you?"
    The glow floated up to the bow.  Cornellius left his wheel and staggered over to the front of the ship.  His movements wobbled as the ship was slapped back and forth.  As he approached, more of the glow's shape was visible.  It was a woman with blue emissive markings carved into her body.  Crabs, squid, and starfish crawled over her body forming clothes along with the barnacle and coral that grew out of her skin.  "Who seeks me?", she called out.

    <!-- TODO: Dialogue
        Have more dialogue between Corny and the Creature
        Maybe have Corny more enamored with the Creature or more horrified
    -->

    "My name is Cornellius Bloomberg, I have been lost for 20 years.  My body's frail, it's been barely sustained by ocean scraps, and it longs for home!", he cried.
    She smirked at him, "Is that your wish Corny?  To go home?"
    "Yes!!"
    "I will grant it, once you rescue us from this cataclysm."
    The water sloshed against his legs as he ran back toward the wheel.

# Chapter 3: The Storm

> Adagio for Strings - Samuel Barber

    Lightning cracked, boring a hole through the ship.  The Creature floated around Cornellius with a look of curiosity.  He grunted as he struggle to steer the ship, fighting against the waves that continued to take bites out of the hull and deck.
    "We're almost there Corny.", the Creature whispered into his ear.
    Sunlight broke through the clouds, a clearing of respite lied ahead of Cornellius.  "I'm almost home."

    <!-- TODO: Action
        Add more action and tension
        More storm
        Show more of the Creature
    -->

    A final wave pushed the ship into the light and the water under the boat had calmed.  "You have rescued me!  As promised, I will grant your wish."
    "Please Djinn, let me come home."
    "This is your only wish Corny, spend it carefully now," the Creature warned.
    "That's all I want!  I wish to go back to never leave again!", he begged.
    The Creature frowned at him, "Your wish is my command."
    Suddenly Cornellius was inside an house.  Cobwebs had moved into every corner and crevice available.  He breathed out and smiled.
    Several growls emanated from behind him and his face dropped.  As Cornellius turned around, he saw a hundred cats stood with arched backs and stoop up hair all hissing and growling.  He hand reached to the scar over his eye and every single feline leaped at him.
